## The Problem: Object Reference Equality

React uses **shallow comparison** (`===`) to determine if props changed. When you pass an object as a prop, React compares **references**, not values.

```javascript
// This creates a NEW object on every render, even if values are identical
<MyComponent config={{ theme: 'dark', size: 'large' }} />
```

Every parent render creates a new object literal with a new memory reference, so `===` always returns `false`, triggering a re-render.

---

## Why Common Fixes Fail

```javascript
// ❌ Still broken - new object reference every render
const MyParent = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <MyChild config={{ theme: 'dark' }} /> // New reference each time
  );
};

// ❌ memo doesn't help here either - shallow comparison still fails
const MyChild = React.memo(({ config }) => <div>{config.theme}</div>);
```

---

## Solutions

### 1. Move static objects outside the component
```javascript
// ✅ Defined once, reference never changes
const CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

const MyParent = () => {
  return <MyChild config={CONFIG} />;
};
```

### 2. useMemo for derived/dynamic objects
```javascript
const MyParent = ({ userId }) => {
  // ✅ Only creates new reference when userId changes
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme: 'dark',
    userId,
    size: 'large'
  }), [userId]);

  return <MyChild config={config} />;
};
```

### 3. Pass primitive props instead of objects
```javascript
// ✅ Primitives compare by value, not reference
const MyParent = () => {
  return <MyChild theme="dark" size="large" />;
};

const MyChild = React.memo(({ theme, size }) => (
  <div className={theme}>{size}</div>
));
```

### 4. useCallback for function props (same problem applies)
```javascript
const MyParent = () => {
  // ✅ Stable function reference
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log('clicked');
  }, []); // Empty deps = never recreated

  return <MyChild onClick={handleClick} />;
};
```

---

## Making React.memo Work Correctly

```javascript
// ✅ Custom comparison function as a last resort
const MyChild = React.memo(
  ({ config }) => <div>{config.theme}</div>,
  (prevProps, nextProps) => {
    // Return true if props are equal (skip re-render)
    return (
      prevProps.config.theme === nextProps.config.theme &&
      prevProps.config.size === nextProps.config.size
    );
  }
);
```

> ⚠️ Custom comparators are error-prone and hard to maintain. Prefer the earlier solutions.

---

## Quick Decision Guide

| Situation | Solution |
|-----------|----------|
| Object never changes | Move outside component |
| Object depends on props/state | `useMemo` |
| Function prop | `useCallback` |
| Can split into primitives | Pass individual props |
| Complex object, last resort | Custom `React.memo` comparator |

---

## Key Takeaway

The root cause is almost always **unnecessary object/function recreation**, not a problem with `React.memo` itself. Fix the reference instability first, then use `memo` to prevent re-renders.
